f 17592186045443] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073753 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b4352698-7af7-5d74-9c3a-0f951c3e6078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186074657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073923 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7809af08-83ac-5450-986d-2614a3fd22d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074657 #edb/ref 1061 197419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "413b9b90-7693-5568-a868-970271a5a258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074658 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074658 #edb/ref 1061 197420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074658 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99e9f36f-648c-5025-bfca-c63f6f77d9b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074658 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074659 #edb/ref 1061 197424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30b3f613-45c5-50c5-bd79-5c12149aea27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075033 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to solve, *without* using graphs, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=4 \\\\ x-2 &=y\\,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order to solve the system without a graph, we will need to combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation.\n\nOne way to combine the equations is called the **substitution method.** To use the substitution method, we substitute for one variable in terms of the other.\n\nFor instance, notice that we can make $y$ the subject of the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\color{red}y=x - 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression for $y$ into the first equation, we get an equation that we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =4 \\\\ x+(x-2) & =4 \\\\ 2x-2 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=3.$ But we also need to figure out what $y$ is. We can do this by substituting $x=3$ back into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-2 \\\\ y & =3-2 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 3$ and $y = 1$, which can be written as $\\left(3, 1\\right).$\n\n**Note:** Whenever we isolate the variable in one equation, we have to substitute it into the *other* equation. We cannot substitute a variable into the same equation it came from, because this will not give us any more information about the variable." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186075033 #edb/ref 1065 8131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075033 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e1e8cb2d-3d4e-5cb9-8947-2bd9deb11b89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075033 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110790 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110790 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110790 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110790 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Congruent Polygons on a Grid" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110790 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110790 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2e2f2884-829b-5e09-8fc0-16556b177732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110791 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110791 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Congruent Polygons Given Side and Angle Measures" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110791 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110791 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "30fc8daf-a6eb-537b-b19b-06ad5504b5e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110792 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110792 #edb/ref 1039 "Writing a Congruence Statement" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110792 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110792 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "46dbecc9-70e8-55c9-b7d7-7b5629ea1549" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110793 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110793 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110793 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Unknown Quantities From Congruent Polygons" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110793 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110793 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0921774a-ff9d-5760-93eb-13517043d509" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110868 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110868 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110868 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110868 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110868 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110868 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110868 #edb/ref 1045 "Congruent Polygons" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110868 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110868 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110868 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110868 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0008051f-7603-507f-b160-f673aeb16997" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111783 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111783 #edb/ref 1061 9553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111783 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a10c82d4-3ea7-5197-929f-a0a7a5910276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111783 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111784 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111784 #edb/ref 1061 9554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111784 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2fa4b989-a0cc-59a1-af0b-34fffe8e4efd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111784 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111785 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111785 #edb/ref 1061 9559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111785 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b16a667-2831-57ad-9ccc-a74ad42b6006" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111785 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111786 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111786 #edb/ref 1061 9560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111786 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "904ecf87-997e-5efd-aa14-ee221a6d6685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111786 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111787 #edb/ref 1061 9561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111787 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c961c803-6d65-5690-a563-dc412e97f007" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111787 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111851 #edb/ref 1064 "Two polygons are **congruent** if they have the same shape. This means that\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nConsider the two polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ below. Are they congruent?\n\n![](images/b0/b0a11815bf9006ccdca0ebe71ff7ba53ac7526c71a715ece71914b103b4a24a8.png)\n\nLet's check these criteria for our polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}.$ We can see that both polygons have $3$ sides, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{AB} & ≅\\overset{―}{XY}\\,, & \\overset{―}{AC} & ≅\\overset{―}{XZ}\\,, & \\overset{―}{BC} & ≅\\overset{―}{YZ}\\,, \\\\ ∠X & ≅∠A\\,, & ∠Y & ≅∠B\\,, & ∠Z & ≅∠C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygons are congruent, and we can write \n\n$$\nABC \\cong XYZ.\n$$\n\nThe order in which we write the symbols is important: the corresponding vertices have to be in the same order.\n\nWe can also write $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{Q}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111851 #edb/ref 1065 2293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111851 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e6be6893-a7ca-578a-9510-8a8c2166e89b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111851 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186