1045 "Modeling Exponential Growth With Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186148356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147671 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b830a80c-d680-54e0-801a-894c774ffea4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148356 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148356 #edb/ref 1061 531824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148356 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "642a7826-566a-5e56-b810-cb6fb7d5fa0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148356 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148357 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148357 #edb/ref 1061 531825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148357 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0ba8192-ba62-514c-b119-d4b80274d19c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148357 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148358 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148358 #edb/ref 1061 531828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148358 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4ad684e1-3cee-5aab-8d3d-e3b084e25e37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148358 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148359 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148359 #edb/ref 1061 531829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148359 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c9b07cd7-55e0-5b22-8e5e-94373891336a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148359 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148360 #edb/ref 1061 531832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148360 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5e381067-3801-5773-b9a5-d5c897aa20dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148360 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148535 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148535 #edb/ref 1061 72289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148535 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e956a0b5-6283-59e2-b00b-6fff43cb6aaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148535 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148536 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148536 #edb/ref 1061 72290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148536 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20f09f61-d500-5013-be0d-df259b534912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148536 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148537 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148537 #edb/ref 1061 72293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148537 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "58253af5-26fc-5261-b61b-97788f445428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148537 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148538 #edb/ref 1061 72296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148538 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9f86a1a0-8db6-5c95-9948-5487d96d589a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148538 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148683 #edb/ref 1064 "When dealing with exponential growth, it's more common to be given a **growth rate** instead of a growth factor. A growth rate is usually expressed as a percent.\n\nFor example, suppose that an investor invests $\\[math]500$ dollars in the shares of some high-growth companies. It's expected that the investment will grow by $35\\%$ each year. Can we write a function that describes the value of the investment after $t$ years? \n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=\\[/math]500$ is the **initial value** of the investment\n\n- $r = 0.35$ is the **growth rate** (this is simply $35\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t$ is the time (in years)\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the value of the investment after $t$ years. The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our values for $a$ and $r,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1+0.35)^t.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we arrive at\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1.35)^t.\n$$\n\nWhen creating exponential growth functions, the key takeaway is this:\n\n- If you're given a **growth rate** $r$ (usually expressed as a percent), then use the formula $y = a(1+r)^t.$\n\n- If you're given a **growth factor** $b,$ then use the formula $y = ab^t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148683 #edb/ref 1065 1936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148683 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "65e48fd4-d6c3-5e02-aad1-9a8dae46b0bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148683 #edb/ref 1067 "Modeling Exponential Growth Using a Growth Rate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148684 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that there are $120$ deer in a wildlife reserve. If the deer population doubles every year, can we find a function that gives the number of deer after $t$ years? \n\nFirst, let's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 120$ is the **initial number** of deer\n\n- $b =2$ is the **growth factor** (equal to $2$ because the number of deer doubles every year)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in years)\n\nWe let $y(t)$ be the number of deer in the reserve after $t$ years.  The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $a$ and $b,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 120\\cdot 2^t.\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to find the number of deer in the reserve for any value of $t \\geq 0.$ For example, to find the number of deer after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(3) & =120\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =960\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there will be $960$ deer after $3$ years.\n\nFinally, notice that substituting $t=0$ into the formula gives the initial number of deer:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =120\\cdot {2}^{0} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =120\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148684 #edb/ref 1065 1937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148684 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ed1b9eba-931a-5908-99c6-58b738afef7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148684 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148781 #edb/ref 1064 "To find a part given a percent and whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percent} \\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part}\n$$\n\nHowever, we sometimes want to find a whole given a part and a percent. To do this, we can use the relationship between multiplication and division to rewrite the above formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percent}\n$$\n\nLet's use this formula to find the missing number in the following statement:\n![](images/08/082c6ac53fa8de85b739dde0611943e92cb09d74d07378d6a33043d4b231e88d.svg)\n\nIn this case, the part is $540$, and the percent is $30\\%.$ \n\nWe can express $30\\%$ as a fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30\\div 10}{100\\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nPutting these numbers into our formula for the whole, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =\\text{part}÷\\text{percent} \\\\  & =540÷30% \\\\  & =540÷\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =540\\times \\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{540}{3}\\times 10 \\\\  & =180\\times 10 \\\\  & =1,800\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1,800.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148781 #edb/ref 1065 8170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148781 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "69026176-778a-58ea-8ccf-185968679bd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148781 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186497409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152142 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Constant of Proportionality" 